Id: pass023i
Severity: INFO
Category: accounts
Check: check_passwdspec
Fix: Where policy follows the CIS benchmarks, set PASS_MAX_DAYS 365 or less (and PASS_MIN_DAYS 1, PASS_WARN_AGE 7) in /etc/login.defs.
References: login.defs(5); NIST SP 800-63B, 5.1.1.2; CIS Benchmarks (Ubuntu, RHEL), password expiration
Controls: CIS 5.2; NIST IA-5(1); ISO 5.17; CE User access control

PASS_MAX_DAYS in login.defs is -1 or 10000 days or more (99999
is every distribution's default), so useradd gives new accounts
passwords that never expire.

This is reported as INFO, not as a weakness, on purpose. NIST SP
800-63B says verifiers should not (since its fourth revision, shall
not) make users change passwords periodically, only when there is
evidence they are compromised: forced rotation leads to predictable
passwords. The CIS benchmarks for Ubuntu and RHEL still ask for
PASS_MAX_DAYS of 365 or less, a PASS_MIN_DAYS of at least 1 and a
PASS_WARN_AGE of at least 7, so an audit against them counts this as a
gap. Existing accounts keep their own ageing (chage -l); pass019w names
each one whose password never expires. With expiry on, check_passwdspec
reports settings that undo it as pass024w.
